s to the emergency department with weakness of his right arm' (score=0.80):]\n  Source: Superior pulmonary sulcus tumors and Pancoast's syndrome. > Etiology\n  Pancoast syndrome, the characteristic constellation of shoulder and arm pain, Horner syndrome, and hand muscle weakness, is most commonly caused by apical lung cancer but is not exclusive to this etio\n[RAG context for 'A 55-year-old male bodybuilder presents to the emergency department with weakness of his right arm' (score=0.80):]\n  Source: Introduction to chest wall reconstruction: anatomy and physiology of the chest and indications for chest wall reconstruction. > Clinical Significance\n  A Pancoast tumor is a malignant tumor of the apex of the lung. The tumor causes Horner syndrome and may be involved in the genesis of thoracic outlet syndrome.[18][19][20][21]\n[RAG context for 'The patient states he has experienced these symptoms for a few weeks' (score=0.66):]\n  Source: Superior pulmonary sulcus tumors and Pancoast's syndrome. > Etiology\n  Pancoast syndrome, the characteristic constellation of shoulder and arm pain, Horner syndrome, and hand muscle weakness, is most commonly caused by apical lung cancer but is not exclusive to this etio\n[RAG context for 'The patient states he has experienced these symptoms for a few weeks' (score=0.71):]\n  Source: Management of Lung Cancer Invading the Superior Sulcus. > Etiology\n  Other than bronchogenic carcinomas, malignancies such as primary adenoid cystic carcinomas, thyroid carcinomas, lymphomas, or metastasis from any primary carcinoma or even benign tumors occupying the \n[Indirect reasoning chains (clinical inference):]\n  progressive right arm weakness over weeks → steroid-induced myopathy → Steroid-Induced Brachial Plexopathy\n    (frequency: occasional)\n    Suggestion: Evaluate for steroid-induced myopathy as a potential underlying mechanism, given the patient's anabolic steroid use and chronic progressive weakness\n  17-pound weight loss over 1 mont